"""Curated project handbook. Technical plan, not robot control software."""
PAGES={}
def page(key,title,lead,body): PAGES[key]={'title':title,'lead':lead,'body':body}
page('index','A small farm, built one working loop at a time.','The current plan in one place: start a paper-wick growing trial in New York, then connect and commission the robot in Japan.', '''
<div class="actions"><a class="button" href="ny-trial.html">Start the New York trial →</a><a class="button secondary" href="planters.html">Understand the printed kits</a></div>
<div class="grid three"><article class="card"><span class="badge">Now · NY</span><h3>Grow something this week</h3><p>Garden cress + household paper + water. No robot, sensors, soldering or printed table needed to begin.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge">Next · Japan</span><h3>Commission one care cycle</h3><p>Park the robot. Inspect, measure, make a supervised bottle pour, verify the outcome and recover from interruptions.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge">Later · gated</span><h3>Earn more autonomy</h3><p>Task demonstrations, held-out evaluation, longer observation windows and only then additional care or mobility.</p></article></div>
<h2>What we have decided</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Current direction</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Watering</td><td>A small robot-held pouring bottle. Human refill.</td><td>Chosen; grip/spout and accurate pours untested.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Growing</td><td>Rectangular wicking planter and square covered germination kit. Each can finish a crop.</td><td>Prints in progress; leak/wick tests pending.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sensing</td><td>One removable moisture tool; one removable upward-facing light paddle.</td><td>Selected; thin-paper contact and moving cable need tests.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Use the robot’s head/wrist cameras initially.</td><td>No separate overhead camera in the first build.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Computer</td><td>The Mac connects to robot interfaces, cameras and ESP32 through the owned powered USB hub.</td><td>Physical connections still to commission.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Furniture</td><td>Free fully printed modular table for the planters; separate cart for the robot.</td><td>Table chosen; editable model files still missing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Software</td><td>Persistent care records, validity checks, restart recovery, exception viewer and replay.</td><td>Roadmap only; not implemented by this website.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<figure><img src="assets/assembly-v3.png" alt="Robot cart beside a modular table carrying the two planter kits"><figcaption>Actual planter meshes; conceptual table/robot placement. Bottle spout is a placeholder. Height, reach and structural capacity are untested.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The next three useful actions</h2><ol><li><a href="ny-trial.html#start">Finish the small planter parts, leak-test and sow cress.</a> Seeds added to the existing cart; purchase is still yours to complete.</li><li><a href="prints.html#table">Download the table’s model files.</a> The ZIP received contains Prusa MINI G-code, not the 13 editable 3MF models.</li><li><a href="software.html">Build the simulated evidence/recovery loop before travel.</a> Physical robot commissioning remains September 30.</li></ol>
<div class="callout"><strong>Removed from V0:</strong> electric watering pump, external reservoir, hoses/float switches, scale hardware, separate overhead camera and leak pads. The requested silicone mat, adhesive rubber sheets, plant labels and measuring syringes remain out. Use household measuring materials.</div>
<p class="small">This handbook resolves newer decisions over the original deck/redline. “Selected”, “printed”, “connected” and “validated” mean different things; every unresolved fit is listed where it matters. Last consolidated September 22, 2026.</p>
''')
page('ny-trial','Six days is enough to learn a lot.','Aim to see cress germinate, establish green seedlings, and prove that the printed parts hold and deliver water. A complete harvest is a bonus, not the acceptance test.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#timing">Timing</a><a href="#start">Start today</a><a href="#daily">Daily care</a><a href="#trouble">Troubleshooting</a><a href="#journal">Trial notebook</a></div>
<section id="timing"><h2>The timing works if we start promptly</h2><div class="callout"><strong>Seed packet added:</strong> <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D1ZDLH5L">Organo Republic curled garden cress, 5 g / 2,150 seeds, $4.99</a>, quantity one. Amazon displayed September 23, 7–11 AM in the cart on September 22. Not purchased; recheck delivery at checkout. Seed arrival tomorrow + prompt plate changes leaves roughly 4–5 growing days before a six-days-away departure.</div><p>Garden cress (<em>Lepidium sativum</em>) suits a small paper-based trial. A seed supplier gives roughly <strong>2–4 days to germinate and 7–10 days to harvest</strong>; real timing varies. By departure, look for emerging roots, shoots and opening seed leaves. <a href="https://seedmart.com.au/how-to-grow-cress-microgreens-step-by-step-guide-for-beginners/">Seedmart’s cress guide</a>. This timeline is our planning estimate, not a guarantee for the packet in the cart.</p><p><strong>Printing:</strong> the outer tray was 92% complete at about 11:01 ET on September 22. The two remaining prepared plates total 16h14m before cooling/bed changes. If started promptly after each clear-bed check, completion on September 23 is plausible. If prints slip, sow a separate small reference patch in a kitchen dish; don’t wait for the table. Do not transplant an established rooted paper sheet merely to meet the schedule.</p><p><strong>Purchase cutoff:</strong> tomorrow delivery leaves a useful trial. Arrival September 25 or later gives much less time; if checkout slips that far, skip this online order for the pre-trip test and source seeds locally. The $19.58 growing mats show September 30 delivery and are unnecessary for this trial.</p></section>
<section id="start"><h2>Your first setup, step by step</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Use what you already have</h3><ul><li>Completed printed cress planter, or the square kit.</li><li>Fresh garden-cress seed. Use untreated seed intended for growing; check the delivered packet.</li><li>Plain, unscented household kitchen paper and clean water.</li><li>A clean kitchen tray/dish underneath, a small cup or spoon, and a phone camera.</li><li>A household loose cover if the cress planter needs humidity retention; square kit includes its cover.</li></ul><p>No growing soil, fertilizer, purchased mats, sensors or robot needed for this short paper trial.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>What “self-watering” means</h3><p>The rectangular planter stores a little water below the seeds. Paper carries it upward by capillary action. You still check it and refill by hand.</p><p>The square kit has a cover and removable insert, but it does not automatically wick unless its growing medium makes the needed connection to water.</p><p><a href="planters.html">See both water paths →</a></p></article></div>
<div class="steps"><div class="step"><h3>Inspect, rinse and leak-test</h3><p>Remove loose plastic, rinse with cool water and check the fit without forcing clips. Put the empty trough/outer tray on dry kitchen paper inside an existing dish. Add a modest amount of water below the growing surface. Inspect after 30 minutes and again later that day. Any wet patch underneath fails the test: use another dish for growing while repairing/reprinting.</p><p class="small">The 30-minute check is our proposed initial screen, not a leak certification. PLA prints may retain residue and are not established food-contact equipment. Treat unknown-filament prints as growth/mechanical tests; use a suitable food-growing container for any batch intended to eat. Avoid boiling water or a hot dishwasher on the PLA parts.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Make the wick actually touch the water</h3><p>For the rectangular planter: snap its four insets into the holder. Feed paper down into each inset and lay 3–4 layers across the seed platform, maintaining a continuous wet-paper path. Pre-wet the paper, drain surface puddles and fill the trough below the seed platform. The creator suggests about 80% full; don’t use that as an excuse to submerge seeds.</p><p>For the square kit: put damp paper on the perforated insert inside its outer tray. Keep it moist from above initially; only rely on bottom water after confirming paper/root contact or a working wick. Water underneath an air gap cannot wet the paper automatically.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Sow a small, even patch</h3><p>Scatter dry cress seeds onto moist paper, with no thick piles. Do not pre-soak cress: it becomes sticky when wet. One packet is enough for small test patches; there is no need to densely fill the entire large tray. Give both kits similar seeded patch areas and density, using the same seed lot. Keep an unseeded edge on the same sheet for moisture checks.</p><p>Use a phone note or tape already at home to label A = wick planter, B = square tray, and optionally C = household dish. A household reference can help distinguish seed trouble from a printed-container problem; it is not a controlled scientific experiment.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Keep the first stage damp, then give it light</h3><p>Follow the seed packet’s conditions. A loose cover can reduce early drying; check at least morning and evening and do not seal it airtight. Remove the cover when shoots emerge and begin standing up; use the plant’s condition rather than an automatic date.</p><p>Then place it in bright natural light away from intense window heat, radiators and cold drafts. An ordinary comfortable room is a practical starting point. Leave airflow around the seedlings. Never leave a covered tray baking in direct sun.</p></div></div>
<p class="small">Assembly steps adapted from <a href="https://www.printables.com/model/434505-self-watering-cress-or-microgreens-planter">Macce’s planter instructions</a>; cress sowing/early growth from <a href="https://seedmart.com.au/how-to-grow-cress-microgreens-step-by-step-guide-for-beginners/">Seedmart</a>. Our leak screens, comparisons and log schedule are proposed tests. The tray setup is an adaptation to the actual printed kit, not a claim that the seed supplier tested these prints.</p></section>
<section id="daily"><h2>A six-day observation plan</h2><div class="day"><h3>Day 0 · fit, water and sow</h3><p>Complete the leak/wick checks, photograph the dry assembly and first sowing. Record crop, date, paper layers, seeded area and starting water level. Measure water with kitchen utensils only if practical; otherwise record “not measured”.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Days 1–2 · check the water path</h3><p>Morning and evening: check the trough, paper at several spots, and the dry surface underneath. Add only enough water to keep paper evenly damp. Photograph from the same angle. Some seed may swell or begin rooting; slower emergence is still possible.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Days 2–4 · look for emergence</h3><p>When shoots establish, uncover and give bright light. Log first roots, upright shoots and seed leaves. If a patch is dry while the trough has water, fix wick contact instead of adding more water blindly.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Days 4–5 · test useful repeatability</h3><p>Compare wetness across the tray, count manual top-ups and note any leak or wilt. Practice tiny hand pours at the refill opening using your kitchen measure. Do not deliberately drought-stress the live crop; test dry/damp/wet sensor samples separately if electronics are available.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Day 6 · record and close the trial</h3><p>Take final photos, compare the two kits, list changes for Japan and export the notebook. Arrange continued human care in New York or end the trial and clean/dry the prints. Do not pack live seedlings, used growing medium or remaining seed for Japan under an assumption that they are cleared.</p></div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Success before departure:</strong> visible germination, a reliable water path, no observed leaks, a known manual refill routine and photos/notes explaining failures. This does not establish autonomous care, edible-product safety or a guaranteed number of unattended days.</div></section>
<section id="trouble"><h2>What a problem looks like</h2><details><summary>There is water below, but the paper is dry</summary><p>The paper may not reach the reservoir, the wick may have separated, or the tray may be tilted. Re-wet and reconnect it. Check multiple points. Don’t drown the seeds to compensate for an air gap.</p></details><details><summary>Seeds are floating or the surface is puddled</summary><p>Remove excess water gently and lower the water level. Keep the paper damp, not submerged. Avoid blasting seeds with a direct pour.</p></details><details><summary>The shoots are pale or leaning</summary><p>Uncover after emergence and improve light without overheating. Use the same camera angle to track changes; brightness in a phone photo alone is not a light-meter reading.</p></details><details><summary>Nothing has emerged by day 4</summary><p>Check seed identity/packet guidance, freshness, paper moisture and temperature. Compare the household reference if you started one. Don’t keep increasing water; saturated seeds can also fail.</p></details><details><summary>Slime, fuzzy growth or an unpleasant smell</summary><p>Cress produces clear gel around wet seeds. Root hairs can look fuzzy, while spreading fuzzy patches, discoloration or a bad smell warrant stopping and discarding the affected trial. A photo alone may not distinguish them reliably. Don’t eat an uncertain batch; clean the container before restarting.</p></details></section>
<section id="journal"><h2>Your trial notebook</h2><p>Checks and observations stay in this browser. They are not uploaded, synced, or sent to the robot. Export before changing devices or clearing browser storage.</p><label for="trial-start">Sowing date</label><input id="trial-start" type="date"><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="leak">Leak check passed at the tested water level</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="wick">Paper stayed damp through an observed interval</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="sprout">First sprouts photographed</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="light">Cover removed after emergence; seedlings in light</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="handoff">End-of-trial care or cleanup arranged</label><form id="journal-form"><div class="grid"><div><label for="kit">Kit</label><select id="kit" name="kit"><option>Wicking planter A</option><option>Square tray B</option><option>Household reference C</option></select></div><div><label for="stage">Growth stage</label><select id="stage" name="stage"><option>Pre-sowing test</option><option>Seeds sown</option><option>Roots emerging</option><option>Shoots upright</option><option>Green seed leaves</option><option>Problem / trial ended</option></select></div><div><label for="moisture">Paper condition</label><select id="moisture" name="moisture"><option>Evenly damp</option><option>Dry patches</option><option>Puddled / too wet</option><option>Not checked</option></select></div><div><label for="water">Water added (mL, leave blank if unmeasured)</label><input id="water" name="water" type="number" min="0" step="0.1"></div></div><label for="notes">Observation / photo filename</label><textarea id="notes" name="notes" rows="3" maxlength="3000" placeholder="What changed? Where is the photo saved?"></textarea><div class="actions"><button type="submit">Save observation</button><button type="button" id="export-notes">Export notebook JSON</button></div></form><p id="save-status" class="small" role="status">Stored locally in this browser only.</p><div id="journal-entries"></div></section>
''')
page('planters','Two kits. Two water paths.','They are alternatives, not a compulsory germinate-then-transplant sequence. Keep the crop in whichever kit you sowed it in.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#wick">Rectangular planter</a><a href="#square">Square kit</a><a href="#robot">Robot access</a></div>
<section id="wick"><h2>Rectangular self-watering cress planter</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="badge">6 printed pieces</span><h3>What each piece does</h3><ul><li><strong>Trough:</strong> stores the small supply of water.</li><li><strong>Holder:</strong> supports the growing paper above it.</li><li><strong>Four insets:</strong> hold paper down into water and make wick paths. They are not four separate crop pots.</li></ul><p>Trough envelope from the downloaded STL: approximately 180 × 85 × 25 mm. Paper and water are not printed parts.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Capillary action does the work</h3><p>A continuous sheet of paper carries water from the trough to the seed platform. Four insets keep the paper in contact with water.</p><p>Refill before the wick loses contact. Passive watering reduces intervention; it does not guarantee the planter can be left alone for six days.</p></article></div><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-frame wide" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable diagram"><img src="assets/wick-water-path.svg" alt="Cross-section of the planter showing paper wicks, seedlings, water and refill access"></div><figcaption>Water rises through the paper; seeds stay above the stored water. <a href="assets/wick-water-path.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full-size diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure><p><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/434505-self-watering-cress-or-microgreens-planter">Original by Macce · CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. The creator uses household paper and printed PETG; our PLA version needs its own leak and durability checks. <a href="ny-trial.html#start">Follow the start guide</a>.</p></section>
<section id="square"><h2>Square germination tray kit</h2><figure><img src="assets/germination-exploded-v3.png" alt="Three separated parts: cover A, perforated growing insert B and outer water tray C"><figcaption>Original downloaded meshes, separated in Blender to explain their roles. Cover orientation depends on its intended use; use it over the growing insert for early germination.</figcaption></figure><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Function</th><th>Downloaded envelope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A · Cover / part 3</td><td>Retains humidity and blocks light initially; remove for the green growth stage. Weighted blackout is crop-specific and not needed for our first cress trial.</td><td>205 × 205 × 35 mm</td></tr><tr><td>B · Insert / part 2</td><td>Holds medium/seeds; perforations provide drainage/root access.</td><td>195 × 195 × 35 mm</td></tr><tr><td>C · Outer tray / part 1</td><td>Supports the insert and catches or holds water.</td><td>220 × 220 × 50 mm</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These measured STL bounds differ from the listing’s nominal dimensions. Use the downloaded dimensions for print planning. Seed on a suitable moist medium in B; C is not a signal to flood the seeds. If there is an air gap to bottom water, top-wet initially and verify the medium’s water path.</p><p><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/1287698-microgreens-germination-tray-kit">Original by HomestteadAce · CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Both kits can stay on an ordinary stable household surface for the New York trial.</p></section>
<section id="robot"><h2>How the robot joins this later</h2><ol><li>Recognize the tray and the saved watering target while its base is parked.</li><li>Inspect the uncovered crop and test an unseeded moisture patch without digging through roots.</li><li>Use one removable light paddle at plant height as a separate operation.</li><li>Take a small bottle, pour a tested amount at an accessible target and return upright.</li><li>Observe wetness/spills and save evidence. Uncertain delivery pauses the cycle.</li></ol><p>Manual lid removal, bottle refill and tool loading are acceptable V0 steps. The cress refill hole is narrow; the square tray’s access around its fitted insert also needs physical testing. Neither a render nor the upstream demo proves these targets are reachable with our bottle.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Why a water pocket if the robot waters?</strong> The pocket is inside the planter; the robot can refill it. We removed the separate powered irrigation system, not the plant’s ability to hold water.</div></section>
''')
page('prints','Print the kits before the furniture.','K1 Max 994A · 0.4 mm nozzle · PLA loaded. The growing trial can begin on an existing household surface.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#queue">Print queue</a><a href="#table">Modular table</a><a href="#fits">Unfinished attachments</a></div>
<section id="queue"><h2>The prepared three-plate job</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Plate</th><th>Contents</th><th>PLA / estimate</th><th>Last verified status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Germination outer tray, part 1</td><td>309.96 g · 10h10m</td><td>92% on Sep 22 at ~11:01 ET; completion not confirmed.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Germination cover + three cress wick insets</td><td>188.17 g · 6h42m</td><td>Prepared; not started by this update.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Perforated insert + cress trough, holder and final inset</td><td>264.58 g · 9h32m</td><td>Prepared; not started by this update.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>All nine pieces total 762.71 g and about 26h24m of sliced time. Plates 2 + 3 total 16h14m; allow cooling, removal, setup and possible reprints. Each plate needs the previous print removed and the bed checked before starting. No table needs printing for the growing test.</p><p><strong>Recorded settings:</strong> 0.20 mm layers, three walls, five top/bottom layers, 15% infill, no supports; Hyper PLA preset, 220°C nozzle and selected bed preset at 50°C. First-layer perimeter 30 mm/s; outer walls/top surface 80 mm/s. These are the saved job settings, not proof of watertightness. Actual filament brand was not established.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="downloads/farm-two-kits-three-plates.3mf" download>Editable Creality project</a><a class="button secondary" href="downloads/planter-source-stls.zip" download>Original planter STLs + credits</a></div><p>Import with the intended K1 Max profile and inspect before slicing. We do not publish auto-run G-code here. The app showed an “Unknown error” banner alongside normal printing; physical completion/first-layer quality must be checked. Creality AI consent/review was not verified, so AI detection is not counted as a passed print check.</p></section>
<section id="table"><h2>Chosen: Modular Table (100% printed)</h2><p><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/177153-modular-table-100-printed/files">Aj_Awesome1’s free CC0 design</a> uses printed top tiles, rods and snap connectors. This is the selected direction for a full expandable table with legs. The robot remains on its separate cart; the table only carries the growing kits.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>File blocker:</strong> the received <code>print_files.zip</code> contains five pre-sliced Prusa MINI G-code files and a PDF. We still need <strong>ALL MODEL FILES (538 KB)</strong>, the 13 editable 3MF models. Do not run the MINI files on the K1 Max.</div><p>The Blender table is a labeled 640 × 400 × 650 mm layout proxy, not the actual designer’s joints. Final dimensions, connector orientation, wet load, stability, long-term PLA sag and robot reach are untested. Import original parts before creating a real print quantity list. No reliable table filament/time estimate exists yet.</p><figure><img src="assets/assembly-v3.png" alt="Conceptual modular table beside robot cart with actual planter models"><figcaption>Planter geometry is real; table and robot envelopes illustrate placement. A table render is not manufacturing approval.</figcaption></figure><a class="button secondary" href="downloads/farm-modular-table-v3.blend" download>Download current Blender assembly</a></section>
<section id="fits"><h2>Small printed parts still to fit</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Moisture probe holder</h3><p>Carrier and clamp prototypes exist. Add a depth stop referenced to the tray rim and gentle compliance before touching thin paper. Measure the delivered board, gripper and growing depth; don’t assume the rigid prototype is ready.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Light paddle</h3><p>One grippable carrier holds one BH1750 face upward. Keep the sensing face clear of the arm/cable shadow; strain-relieve the moving cable. Tool is parked when watering.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Bottle rest and spout</h3><p>The parked bottle needs repeatable position. The Nalgene wide-mouth container needs a controlled outlet or a replacement household bottle. Shown spout is a placeholder, not an exported working part.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Dry enclosure and locating stops</h3><p>Keep ESP32/boards dry, secure leads and locate the planters repeatably. Cable routes, tool pockets and fasteners depend on measured hardware.</p></article></div><p><a href="downloads/tool-prototypes-v2.zip" download>Download V2 tool prototypes</a> for fit evaluation. The old short pod decks/legs are superseded by the selected full-table direction; they are retained only in the historical design, not part of this print recommendation.</p></section>
''')
page('hardware','One Mac. Separate motor and sensor connections.','The first build keeps the robot parked and uses its existing cameras. Sensors attach through an ESP32; USB does not power the robot’s motors.', '''
<h2>The connection map</h2><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-frame wide" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable diagram"><img src="assets/system-connections.svg" alt="Labeled Mac, hub, robot, camera and sensor connection map"></div><figcaption>Solid lines show data/control; dashed lines show power. USB does not power the robot motors. <a href="assets/system-connections.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full-size diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The project selected the <a href="https://shop.wowrobo.com/products/xlerobot-dual-arm-mobile-household-robot-kit">WowRobo XLeRobot Combo</a>. Recorded kit contents include motor interfaces, three cameras, robot cables and two 12V adapters; verify the delivered boxes and labels. Hub and Anker C300 DC were confirmed bought. The laptop is the current computer plan; no extra Pi is required just to run the sensor logger. Actual macOS drivers, camera bandwidth and robot-software compatibility still need a bench check.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Latest cart gap to resolve by reuse:</strong> the separate USB-A to USB-C data cable is no longer in the active cart. Have a suitable existing data cable ready for the ESP32; a charge-only cable will not carry readings. The cart’s removal does not prove it was purchased. Likewise, arrange existing cable ties/fasteners/insulation as needed.</div>
<h2>Each electrical part, in plain language</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>ESP32: the sensor translator</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-frame"><img src="assets/esp32-usb.svg" alt="Hub USB cable, ESP32 and Mac sensor records"></div><figcaption>One board translates sensor signals into USB serial records. <a href="assets/esp32-usb.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full-size diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>One board reads sensors; the three-pack gives spares. The robot’s motor boards do not replace it. Develop and test the serial protocol on the bench before attaching anything to a moving arm.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Light: four connections</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-frame"><img src="assets/light-wiring.svg" alt="Four labeled light-sensor leads"></div><figcaption>Dashed lead: supply. Solid leads: ground and I²C. <a href="assets/light-wiring.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full-size diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>*Confirm the breakout’s pin labels/supply requirements. Use a 3.3V-compatible bus. Route a flexible four-wire cable to one removable paddle. Test errors through the complete arm motion; long I²C wiring is not automatically reliable.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Moisture: local wetness</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-frame"><img src="assets/moisture-wiring.svg" alt="Three labeled moisture-probe leads"></div><figcaption>Signal is read by the ESP32; power is switched separately. <a href="assets/moisture-wiring.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full-size diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>The SparkFun SEN-13637 measures conductivity, affected by contact and dissolved salts. It is a local wetness proxy, not a reservoir level meter. Keep signal within the ESP32 input range; never feed 5V into its ADC.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Relay: briefly power the probe</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-frame"><img src="assets/relay-control.svg" alt="Relay module control, supply and ground connections"></div><figcaption>Verify the module supply and trigger configuration. <a href="assets/relay-control.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full-size diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-frame"><img src="assets/relay-contacts.svg" alt="Open relay contact between 3.3V supply and probe VCC"></div><figcaption>The relay contact powers only the probe. <a href="assets/relay-contacts.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full-size diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>Use one relay channel; no pump is switched. Verify the delivered module’s trigger configuration, supply current and boot-off behavior. Contact side switches 3.3V; relay coil supply is separate. Final GPIOs and power pin selection wait for board pinout verification.</p></article></div>
<p class="small">These are functional wiring diagrams, not an approved pin-by-pin harness. Read the board labels and <a href="https://learn.sparkfun.com/tutorials/soil-moisture-sensor-hookup-guide/all">SparkFun hookup guide</a>; check voltage/continuity before plugging the sensor harness into the computer.</p>
<h2>What needs soldering?</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Soldering task</th><th>What connects afterward</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESP32 / BH1750 / optional SHT31</td><td>If headers arrive loose, solder the header pins into the board’s plated holes. Already fitted headers need none.</td><td>Jumper plugs for bench tests; retained connectors for final build.</td></tr><tr><td>Perfboard + terminal kit</td><td>Solder headers and screw-terminal pins to perfboard, plus planned interconnects.</td><td>Strip wire ends and clamp under terminal screws.</td></tr><tr><td>Screw-terminal moisture probe</td><td>No solder at the probe terminal if the block arrives fitted.</td><td>Three wire ends into VCC/GND/SIG.</td></tr><tr><td>Moving wire splices</td><td>Only if extending/joining wires that way.</td><td>Insulate exposed joints with heat-shrink or use suitable enclosed connectors.</td></tr><tr><td>USB cables, robot kit leads, bottle, planters</td><td>No added soldering for their ordinary connections.</td><td>Plug-in or mechanical assembly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Calibration and contact testing</h2><p><strong>Calibration</strong> means recording what your installed probe reads on the actual paper with known dry, damp and wet examples. Repeat placements, save the raw range and define valid/ambiguous readings. Do not invent a universal percentage or label voltage as volumetric soil moisture.</p><p><strong>Contact testing</strong> means proving the probe really touches the intended patch consistently without hitting the plastic or crushing the crop. Include “hovering/not touching” trials: they must be INVALID, not interpreted as dry. A compliant printed holder/depth stop helps; software cannot correct a probe that misses the paper.</p><p>For light, extend the paddle to canopy height, face upward, avoid shadows, stop moving and collect fresh conversions. Start with roughly ten readings over two seconds at default timing, logging median/spread and pose. Reject stale/saturated/bus-error samples. Repeated readings improve repeatability, not absolute calibration; lux is not PPFD. <a href="https://www.mouser.com/datasheet/2/348/bh1750fvi-e-186247.pdf">ROHM datasheet</a>.</p>
<h2>Power and cameras still need commissioning</h2><p>Start from the robot’s supplied wall adapters; check that the actual labels accept Japan’s 100V mains. The owned 288Wh C300 DC is not an already completed motor-power solution: its connector, voltage/current capacity and protection path must match the robot. Do not assume USB-C alone supplies fixed 12V motor power.</p><p>A fixed overview camera would need its own mount and cable path back to a computer; a mobile robot cannot remain tethered to a fixed camera indefinitely. Our initial choice avoids that extra camera: use the existing head/wrist views and validate that they cover the watering area. No Raspberry Pi, depth camera, scale or pump purchase is justified by this diagram.</p>
''')
page('software','Build a care cycle that can explain what happened.','The redline feedback becomes an observable, recoverable loop. These are proposed milestones; this site does not implement robot software.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#cycle">Care cycle</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a><a href="#schedule">Schedule</a><a href="#models">Jev / Astra</a><a href="#gates">Release gates</a></div>
<section id="cycle"><h2>Start with plants staying in place</h2><ol class="visual-flow" aria-label="Proposed care cycle"><li><span class="flow-number">01</span><strong>Identify</strong><span>Confirm the tray</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">02</span><strong>Inspect</strong><span>Fresh camera evidence</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">03</span><strong>Measure</strong><span>Valid contact and reading</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">04</span><strong>Decide</strong><span>Check care prerequisites</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">05</span><strong>Bottle care</strong><span>Supervised, bounded pour</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">06</span><strong>Verify</strong><span>Record the actual outcome</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">07</span><strong>Park tool</strong><span>Return to a known rest</span></li></ol><div class="recovery-path"><strong>Pause / needs review</strong><p>Any unresolved condition pauses the cycle. On restart, reconcile the physical state before choosing an eligible next step.</p></div><p>The feedback’s pick/dock/weigh/dose/return sequence described a broader station design. In our bottle-and-in-place profile, pod handling is explicitly not applicable and scale/pump adapters stay disabled. If we later move pods, reinstate those states with independent grip/location evidence.</p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Before a pour</h3><p>Correct tray identity, fresh camera view, valid measurement/contact, known small fill, verified bottle grip, clear reachable target and a tested upright recovery path. A cloud model cannot override any failed prerequisite.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>After a pour</h3><p>A motion acknowledgement proves receipt, not water delivery. Record dose as estimated or human-attested unless independently measured. Unknown delivery pauses; never blindly repeat an uncertain pour.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="evidence"><h2>Six requirements from the feedback</h2><ol><li><strong>Explicit states:</strong> every transition declares evidence, deadline and recovery action.</li><li><strong>Persistent actions:</strong> save cycle/command IDs and INTENDED, ATTEMPTED and VERIFIED separately. Duplicate requests return existing records. A crash during possible watering leaves UNKNOWN until reconciled.</li><li><strong>Valid sensors:</strong> freshness, calibrated range, contact, camera coverage and quality flags are action prerequisites.</li><li><strong>Shared evidence:</strong> link tray, recipe, images, readings, actions, interventions and all relevant versions in one exportable incident.</li><li><strong>Exception viewer:</strong> show the latest image, its age, current action, last verified state and eligible recovery choices. Disable a watering retry when delivery is unknown.</li><li><strong>Fault replay:</strong> simulate interruptions now; retain real failures as regressions later.</li></ol>
<details><summary>Minimal proposed evidence record</summary><pre>{
  "cycle_id": "...", "command_id": "...", "tray_id": "A",
  "source": "simulation | hardware",
  "recipe_version": "...", "time": "...",
  "images": [{"id": "...", "captured_at": "..."}],
  "readings": [{"value": 0, "unit": "raw_adc",
    "validity": "INVALID_CONTACT", "calibration_id": "..."}],
  "action": "water", "state": "INTENDED",
  "delivery": "not_attempted | unknown | human_attested | measured",
  "software_version": "...", "policy_version": "...",
  "interventions": []
}</pre><p>SQLite plus image files is the proposed local record store. Command IDs alone cannot guarantee exactly-once physical watering. Preserve original images and keep simulation evidence distinct from hardware evidence.</p></details></section>
<section id="schedule"><h2>Software now; robot commissioning September 30</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Window</th><th>Deliverable</th><th>Gate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sep 22–24</td><td>Capability profile, event schema, simulator, durable action records, initial exception viewer. Run the separate NY grow trial.</td><td>Interrupt every simulated state; no duplicate or uncertain repeat watering.</td></tr><tr><td>Sep 25–26</td><td>Fault replay, sensor validity, camera spill cases, Jev shadow adapter.</td><td>Stale/hidden view, wrong identity, missing contact, uncertain grip, dropped ACK and cloud loss are handled.</td></tr><tr><td>Sep 27–29</td><td>Freeze crop recipe, export/restore, offline rehearsal and packaging.</td><td>Recover evidence and resolve a defined exception through the viewer. NY seedlings remain with local care or are cleaned up.</td></tr><tr><td>Sep 30–Oct 2 · D1–3</td><td>Hardware adapters, calibration, dummy-tray recovery, moving cable sweeps and small hand/teleop bottle pours.</td><td>Measure real fits, prove safe state-dependent stops and contact validity; do not infer delivered volume from tilt.</td></tr><tr><td>Oct 3–5 · D4–6</td><td>Supervised parked cycles at every intended tray position; collect demonstrations once teleop is reliable.</td><td>All outcomes and interventions recorded. No unobserved watering authority.</td></tr><tr><td>Oct 6–8 · D7–9</td><td>Feature freeze; progressive 2-hour, then 8-hour, then 24-hour observation/alert windows, with human watering arranged.</td><td>Relevant faults pass first. Autonomous watering requires a separate validated delivery/recovery profile.</td></tr><tr><td>Oct 9–10 · D10–11</td><td>Repeat weak cases, restore backups, export dataset and caretaker handoff.</td><td>Caretaker can resolve representative exceptions. Navigation remains conditional.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Dates are targets, not evidence of completion. A failed gate holds the previous operating level. The NY growing trial is newly in scope; earlier statements that no physical work happens until September 30 now apply to robot commissioning, not planter tests.</p></section>
<section id="models"><h2>Jev classifies; Astra investigates; local checks decide eligibility</h2><ol class="visual-flow" aria-label="Model review and action workflow"><li><span class="flow-number">01</span><strong>Local evidence</strong><span>Checks and fresh images</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">02</span><strong>Jev</strong><span>Shadow classification</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">03</span><strong>Astra</strong><span>Investigate exceptions</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">04</span><strong>Proposal</strong><span>Review the suggested action</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">05</span><strong>Local controller</strong><span>Act only when eligible</span></li><li><span class="flow-number">06</span><strong>Verify</strong><span>Independent outcome evidence</span></li></ol><p>Jev categories: normal, needs reinspection, handling fault, watering discrepancy, crop concern and unknown. Begin in shadow mode with no actuation permission. The redline’s 0.85 heuristic is not a validated threshold, and the described helper source has not been audited here.</p><p>Compare rules and Jev on the same evidence: missed faults, false clearances, unnecessary alerts, review time, latency and cost. Independently label outcomes and hold out episodes from tuning. The suggested 120 labelled episodes / at least 10 per priority fault class is a starting collection target, not a reliability guarantee.</p><p>Astra may propose changes and experiments. Version and evaluate them before a bounded supervised trial; retain rollback. Automatic code modification/retraining and complex orchestration are deferred.</p></section>
<section id="gates"><h2>Spill checks and failure recovery</h2><p>Camera spill status is CLEAR / SPILL / UNKNOWN. Hidden, dark, stale, glare-obscured or out-of-frame areas are UNKNOWN. Dedicated leak pads were removed at your request; vision remains experimental.</p><p>If a pour must stop, execute the commissioned return-upright action when control is available. Do not blindly release or de-energize a tilted bottle. Total power loss may leave it draining; small starting fills and household containment matter. Do not claim camera software alone prevents damage.</p><p>Every release replays accumulated failures. Hardware changes trigger the relevant real tests again. Mobile navigation, automatic refill, printer part pickup and construction tasks remain outside the first dependable parked loop.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="research.html#upstream">What upstream already provides</a><a class="button secondary" href="downloads/software-roadmap-detail.html">Detailed roadmap companion</a></div></section>
''')
page('japan','Bring the experiment, not a live crop.','Carry-on only, ANA. Use the New York week for evidence; start a fresh local crop after arrival.', '''
<h2>Pack / source / verify</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Plan</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Printed kits and dry electronics</td><td>Clean, dry and protect; keep connections identified.</td><td>Remove growing material and water. Protect sensor ends and loose battery terminals.</td></tr><tr><td>Seed and used paper / soil / plants</td><td>NY test stays in the US; buy Japan seed locally.</td><td>Japan plant quarantine regulates seeds/plants and prohibits soil. We have not obtained import documents.</td></tr><tr><td>Bottles</td><td>Pack empty.</td><td>Refill after arrival; do not carry irrigation water.</td></tr><tr><td>Pointed soldering iron and hand tools</td><td>Borrow / buy in Japan.</td><td>ANA explicitly excludes pointed soldering irons and broadly restricts tools in carry-on.</td></tr><tr><td>Multimeter and pointed probes</td><td>Borrow / buy in Japan unless ANA confirms the exact kit.</td><td>No explicit ANA classification was found. This is uncertainty, not proof every meter is banned.</td></tr><tr><td>Anker C300 DC, 288Wh</td><td>Destination power equipment; do not pack for this passenger flight.</td><td>Exceeds the normal passenger lithium-battery limits. Being owned does not establish motor wiring compatibility.</td></tr><tr><td>Growing mats</td><td>Optional. Use household paper for NY; source locally if needed later.</td><td>Current cart delivery Sep 30 misses the NY trial. Exact processed wood-fiber customs treatment was not individually cleared.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><a href="https://www.ana.co.jp/en/jp/guide/boarding-procedures/baggage/international/caution-restriction02/">ANA tool restrictions</a> · <a href="https://www.ana.co.jp/en/jp/guide/boarding-procedures/baggage/international/caution-restriction03/">ANA conditional items / batteries / soldering irons</a> · <a href="https://www.faa.gov/hazmat/packsafe/batteries">FAA ordinary dry batteries (including 9V)</a> · <a href="https://www.maff.go.jp/pps/j/introduction/english.html">MAFF plant quarantine</a>. Recheck the actual itinerary; codeshare and departure-country rules also apply.</p>
<h2>Before leaving New York</h2><ol><li>Photograph the final crop, water path and any leak/failure. Export the trial notebook.</li><li>Leave an actively growing trial with an informed caretaker, or end it and remove plant material. Do not abandon a filled experimental tray beside electronics.</li><li>Clean and dry prints; record any distortion and snap-fit damage.</li><li>Pack model files, camera images, recipe and calibration notes. Keep data backed up separately.</li><li>Arrange the Japan work surface/cart, seeds, basic tools and suitable electrical supplies. Finish modular-table geometry only after the correct files and reach measurements are available.</li></ol>
<h2>First Japan session</h2><p>Confirm delivered kit contents, power-adapter labels and cable types. Build the dry sensor bench first. Test cameras, serial devices, supply voltages and wiring continuity before wet operation. Then commission dummy trays and very small supervised pours. A successful NY paper crop proves neither robot reach nor unattended watering.</p>
''')
page('research','Reuse the parts that have evidence.','Original models, upstream demonstrations, the experiment design and downloadable project files—without confusing proposals with proven behavior.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#upstream">Existing work</a><a href="#experiment">Crop experiment</a><a href="#downloads">Files</a><a href="#decisions">Decision history</a></div>
<section id="upstream"><h2>What pre-existing work gives us</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Useful contribution</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/demos/index.html">XLeRobot showcase</a></td><td>Plant watering at ~56–59 seconds uses a tipped bottle. Clip labels it “Teleoped with Switch Joycons”.</td><td>Does not establish a ready autonomous plant-watering policy; spray-trigger use was not demonstrated in that segment.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/software/getting_started/VLA_ACT.html">Official ACT guide</a></td><td>Record task demonstrations, train and evaluate; consistent camera views matter.</td><td>We need our own bottle/target data and evaluation. A Mac sensor logger is not proof every training workload runs locally.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/software/getting_started/XLeRobot_teleop.html">Official teleoperation</a></td><td>Starting point for supervised arm motion.</td><td>Control scripts are not crop-care policies.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/434505-self-watering-cress-or-microgreens-planter">Macce cress planter</a></td><td>Printable wick-holder design using household paper.</td><td>Our PLA print still needs fit/leak testing.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/1287698-microgreens-germination-tray-kit">HomestteadAce kit</a></td><td>Outer tray, perforated insert and germination cover.</td><td>Medium contact and watering access depend on our setup.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/177153-modular-table-100-printed/files">Aj_Awesome1 modular table</a></td><td>Free CC0 snap-together furniture parts.</td><td>Editable files not yet obtained; no verified load rating or configured K1 Max print estimate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Prior wider research considered FarmBot, OpenFarm, GreenPiThumb, GardenBot, MudPi and agricultural rovers. They offer useful control/logging or garden-system ideas; none is evidence of a drop-in policy for this particular arm, bottle and paper tray. <a href="downloads/existing-work-historical.md">Read the dated broader research</a>.</p><p>Two water-named datasets inspected in the earlier review describe drink/bottle transport, not irrigation. No ready-to-run plant-watering checkpoint or multi-day care evaluation was located in that bounded review. <a href="downloads/watering-research.md">Source-by-source watering findings</a> · <a href="https://vector-wangel.github.io/XLeRobot-assets/videos/Real_demos/xlerobot030.mp4#t=56,60">Watch the watering clip</a>.</p></section>
<section id="experiment"><h2>Why one crop first?</h2><p>Use the same garden-cress lot for both NY kit tests. This makes water-path and handling problems easier to interpret than a mix of plants with different germination rates. Compare like-sized seeded patches and similar light; different tray geometries mean this remains a practical pilot, not an isolated causal test.</p><p>In Japan, use repeated batches of the same fast crop for robot-assisted versus human-baseline care. Assign comparable positions, keep seed lot/medium/density/recipe consistent, log all interventions, and distinguish tray-level outcomes from individual-seed counts. The value of multiple waves is checking whether a result repeats—not collecting variety for its own sake. Add another species later only to test a specific generalization question.</p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Crop outcomes</h3><p>Emergence timing, visible survival/coverage, photos at fixed positions, healthy seed leaves, moisture consistency and the actual harvest when available.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Robot outcomes</h3><p>Valid sensing, correct target, successful/unknown delivery, spills, recoveries, human interventions and review time. A green crop alone does not prove autonomous care.</p></article></div><p>Use a manual care baseline. Do not withhold necessary water to preserve a comparison; intervene and record it. Six NY days can establish feasibility and expose failures, not prove a statistically reliable autonomy advantage.</p></section>
<section id="downloads"><h2>Downloads and source trail</h2><ul><li><a href="downloads/farm-modular-table-v3.blend">Blender assembly</a> — actual planter meshes, proxy table/robot.</li><li><a href="downloads/planter-source-stls.zip">Planter STL pack + license credits</a> — two creators, nine printed instances from six unique source files.</li><li><a href="downloads/farm-two-kits-three-plates.3mf">Editable Creality three-plate project</a> — inspect profile/settings before slicing.</li><li><a href="downloads/tool-prototypes-v2.zip">Tool-holder prototype STLs</a> — measured fit and probe compliance still pending.</li><li><a href="downloads/software-roadmap-detail.html">Detailed software roadmap</a> — updated current furniture note; engineering proposal.</li><li><a href="data/shopping.json">Shopping snapshot and per-item reasons</a> — last verified Sep 22; no personal items.</li><li><a href="xlerobot-deck.html">Original roadmap deck, archived</a> — older decisions, prominent superseded notice.</li><li><a href="sensor-shopping.html">Original sensor checklist, archived</a> — historical only.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/RonTuretzky/xlerobot-farm">Website source repository</a>.</li></ul><p>Planter models: Macce / HomestteadAce, CC BY-SA 4.0; see <a href="downloads/MODEL-CREDITS.md">model credits</a>. Assembly rendering/arrangement includes those models and is distributed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Original downloadable table selection is CC0. The site includes no raw private conversation transcripts or account addresses.</p></section>
<section id="decisions"><h2>What changed from older plans</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Older idea</th><th>Current decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Likely Bambu A1</td><td>Available printer is Creality K1 Max 994A. Do not use another machine’s pre-sliced files.</td></tr><tr><td>Scale / fixed pump station</td><td>In-place sensing and robot-held bottle. No scale or electric pump in V0.</td></tr><tr><td>External water reservoir</td><td>Small manually refilled bottle and planter’s internal water pocket.</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed overhead camera</td><td>Robot head/wrist cameras first, with verified view coverage.</td></tr><tr><td>Many sensors attached at once</td><td>One task tool at a time; optional air sensor off the arm.</td></tr><tr><td>Low eight-pod stand on furniture</td><td>Two actual downloaded kits; free full modular table selected, exact parts still pending.</td></tr><tr><td>No physical work before travel</td><td>NY print/growing tests now; robot commissioning September 30.</td></tr><tr><td>Autonomous demo already solves watering</td><td>Verified clip is teleoperated; collect/evaluate our own task data.</td></tr><tr><td>Leak pads / extra measuring and grip supplies</td><td>Leak pads deferred, requested mat/rubber/labels/syringes removed. Use household measurement/containment.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The original PowerPoint redline emphasized dependable care cycles, evidence, remote recovery and model evaluation. Its scale/pump-specific suggestions have been translated into the current bottle profile rather than silently treated as shopping requirements.</p></section>
''')

